<!-- doc/src/sgml/auto-explain.sgml -->

<sect1 id="auto-explain" xreflabel="auto_explain">
 <title>auto_explain &mdash; log execution plans of slow queries</title>

 <indexterm zone="auto-explain">
  <primary>auto_explain</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  The <filename>auto_explain</filename> module provides a means for
  logging execution plans of slow statements automatically, without
  having to run <xref linkend="sql-explain"/>
  by hand.  This is especially helpful for tracking down un-optimized queries
  in large applications.
 </para>

 <para>
  The module provides no SQL-accessible functions.  To use it, simply
  load it into the server.  You can load it into an individual session:

<programlisting>
LOAD 'auto_explain';
</programlisting>

  (You must be superuser to do that.)  More typical usage is to preload
  it into some or all sessions by including <literal>auto_explain</literal> in
  <xref linkend="guc-session-preload-libraries"/> or
  <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/> in
  <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>.  Then you can track unexpectedly slow queries
  no matter when they happen.  Of course there is a price in overhead for
  that.
 </para>

 <sect2 id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters">
  <title>Configuration Parameters</title>

 <para>
  There are several configuration parameters that control the behavior of
  <filename>auto_explain</filename>.  Note that the default behavior is
  to do nothing, so you must set at least
  <varname>auto_explain.log_min_duration</varname> if you want any results.
 </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-min-duration">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_min_duration</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_min_duration</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_min_duration</varname> is the minimum statement
      execution time, in milliseconds, that will cause the statement's plan to
      be logged. Setting this to <literal>0</literal> logs all plans.
      <literal>-1</literal> (the default) disables logging of plans. For
      example, if you set it to <literal>250ms</literal> then all statements
      that run 250ms or longer will be logged. Only superusers can change this
      setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-parameter-max-length">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_parameter_max_length</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_parameter_max_length</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_parameter_max_length</varname> controls the
      logging of query parameter values. A value of <literal>-1</literal> (the
      default) logs the parameter values in full. <literal>0</literal> disables
      logging of parameter values. A value greater than zero truncates each
      parameter value to that many bytes. Only superusers can change this
      setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-analyze">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> causes <command>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</command>
      output, rather than just <command>EXPLAIN</command> output, to be printed
      when an execution plan is logged. This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
     <note>
      <para>
       When this parameter is on, per-plan-node timing occurs for all
       statements executed, whether or not they run long enough to actually
       get logged.  This can have an extremely negative impact on performance.
       Turning off <varname>auto_explain.log_timing</varname> ameliorates the
       performance cost, at the price of obtaining less information.
      </para>
     </note>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-buffers">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_buffers</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_buffers</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_buffers</varname> controls whether buffer
      usage statistics are printed when an execution plan is logged; it's
      equivalent to the <literal>BUFFERS</literal> option of <command>EXPLAIN</command>.
      This parameter has no effect
      unless <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> is enabled.
      This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-io">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_io</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_io</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_io</varname> controls whether I/O usage
      statistics are printed when an execution plan is logged; it's
      equivalent to the <literal>IO</literal> option of <command>EXPLAIN</command>.
      This parameter has no effect
      unless <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> is enabled.
      This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-wal">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_wal</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_wal</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_wal</varname> controls whether WAL
      usage statistics are printed when an execution plan is logged; it's
      equivalent to the <literal>WAL</literal> option of <command>EXPLAIN</command>.
      This parameter has no effect
      unless <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> is enabled.
      This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-timing">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_timing</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_timing</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_timing</varname> controls whether per-node
      timing information is printed when an execution plan is logged; it's
      equivalent to the <literal>TIMING</literal> option of <command>EXPLAIN</command>.
      The overhead of repeatedly reading the system clock can slow down
      queries significantly on some systems, so it may be useful to set this
      parameter to off when only actual row counts, and not exact times, are
      needed.
      This parameter has no effect
      unless <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> is enabled.
      This parameter is on by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-triggers">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_triggers</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_triggers</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_triggers</varname> causes trigger
      execution statistics to be included when an execution plan is logged.
      This parameter has no effect
      unless <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> is enabled.
      This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-verbose">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_verbose</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_verbose</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_verbose</varname> controls whether verbose
      details are printed when an execution plan is logged; it's
      equivalent to the <literal>VERBOSE</literal> option of <command>EXPLAIN</command>.
      This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-settings">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_settings</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_settings</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_settings</varname> controls whether information
      about modified configuration options is printed when an execution plan is logged.
      Only options affecting query planning with value different from the built-in
      default value are included in the output.  This parameter is off by default.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-format">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_format</varname> (<type>enum</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_format</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_format</varname> selects the
      <command>EXPLAIN</command> output format to be used.
      The allowed values are <literal>text</literal>, <literal>xml</literal>,
      <literal>json</literal>, and <literal>yaml</literal>.  The default is text.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-extension-options">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_extension_options</varname> (<type>string</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_extension_options</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Loadable modules can extend the <literal>EXPLAIN</literal> command with
      additional options that affect the output format. Such options can also
      be specified here. The value of this parameter is a comma-separated
      list of options, each of which is an option name followed optionally by
      an associated value. The module that provides the
      <literal>EXPLAIN</literal> option, such as
      <link linkend="pgplanadvice"><literal>pg_plan_advice</literal></link> or
      <link linkend="pgoverexplain"><literal>pg_overexplain</literal></link>,
      should be loaded before this parameter is set.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-level">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_level</varname> (<type>enum</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_level</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_level</varname> selects the log level at which
      auto_explain will log the query plan.
      Valid values are <literal>DEBUG5</literal>, <literal>DEBUG4</literal>,
      <literal>DEBUG3</literal>, <literal>DEBUG2</literal>,
      <literal>DEBUG1</literal>, <literal>INFO</literal>,
      <literal>NOTICE</literal>, <literal>WARNING</literal>,
      and <literal>LOG</literal>. The default is <literal>LOG</literal>.
      Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-nested-statements">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_nested_statements</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_nested_statements</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_nested_statements</varname> causes nested
      statements (statements executed inside a function) to be considered
      for logging.  When it is off, only top-level query plans are logged. This
      parameter is off by default. Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-sample-rate">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.sample_rate</varname> (<type>real</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.sample_rate</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.sample_rate</varname> causes auto_explain to only
      explain a fraction of the statements in each session.  The default is 1,
      meaning explain all the queries.  In case of nested statements, either all
      will be explained or none. Only superusers can change this setting.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-log-redact">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname> causes logged plans to be
      redacted: the names of user-defined objects are replaced by
      pseudonyms, constants are replaced by <literal>?</literal>, and the
      query text and parameter values are left out, while the plan shape,
      costs, row counts and timings are kept.  The redaction is the same as
      that of the <literal>REDACT</literal> option of
      <command>EXPLAIN</command>, except that the schemas listed in
      <varname>auto_explain.redact_allow_schemas</varname> are exempted.
      <xref linkend="auto-explain-redaction"/> describes what a redacted plan
      contains.  This parameter is off by default.
     </para>
     <warning>
      <para>
       Only the plans written by <filename>auto_explain</filename> are
       redacted; nothing else that the server writes to the log is affected.
       Statements logged because of <xref linkend="guc-log-statement"/>,
       <xref linkend="guc-log-min-duration-statement"/> or
       <xref linkend="guc-log-min-error-statement"/> are written in full,
       including the text that was removed from the plan.
       <xref linkend="guc-log-line-prefix"/> is expanded as usual, and the
       fields that the <literal>csvlog</literal> and <literal>jsonlog</literal>
       formats attach to every record, such as the user name, database name,
       <varname>application_name</varname> and client address, are written
       unchanged, on the redacted records too.  The exception is the query
       identifier, which is left out of the plan: on the line that carries a
       redacted plan, and on the messages <filename>auto_explain</filename>
       writes to warn about settings that weaken redaction,
       <literal>%Q</literal> and the query identifier field of
       <literal>csvlog</literal> and <literal>jsonlog</literal> show
       <literal>0</literal>.  Every other line shows it as usual, including
       any other message written while the same statement runs.  If
       <xref linkend="guc-log-min-messages"/> is <literal>debug1</literal> or
       lower, the log also receives the text of every statement whose plan
       was redacted (see <xref linkend="auto-explain-redaction"/>).  Review
       these settings before relying on redaction.
      </para>
     </warning>
     <para>
      Unlike the other <filename>auto_explain</filename> parameters, this
      parameter can only be set in the <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>
      file or on the server command line.  If a session could change it, a
      session could turn redaction off for its own statements and write
      unredacted plans into a log that is read by others.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="auto-explain-configuration-parameters-redact-allow-schemas">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_explain.redact_allow_schemas</varname> (<type>string</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_explain.redact_allow_schemas</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>auto_explain.redact_allow_schemas</varname> is a
      comma-separated list of schemas whose objects keep their real names in
      redacted plans, for example the schema of a trusted extension.  The
      names are interpreted as identifiers, so unquoted names are folded to
      lower case.  Objects in other schemas are still redacted, and only
      names are exempted: constants are shown as <literal>?</literal> and
      aliases written in the query are shown as pseudonyms even where they
      refer to an object in a listed schema.  The list has no effect unless
      <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname> is on, and it does not apply
      to <command>EXPLAIN (REDACT)</command>, which exempts only
      <literal>pg_catalog</literal> and <literal>information_schema</literal>.
      The default is empty.
     </para>
     <para>
      Listing a schema exempts every object in it, including objects created
      in it later.  A schema that holds application objects, such as
      <literal>public</literal>, should therefore not be listed.  If
      <literal>public</literal> is listed, <filename>auto_explain</filename>
      writes a message saying so to the log once per session.
     </para>
     <para>
      This parameter can only be set in the <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>
      file or on the server command line, for the same reason as
      <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname>: a session able to change it
      could exempt its own schema.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   In ordinary usage, these parameters are set
   in <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>, although superusers can alter them
   on-the-fly within their own sessions.  The exceptions are
   <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname> and
   <varname>auto_explain.redact_allow_schemas</varname>, which can only be
   set in the configuration file or on the server command line.
   Typical usage might be:
  </para>

<programlisting>
# postgresql.conf
session_preload_libraries = 'auto_explain'

auto_explain.log_min_duration = '3s'
</programlisting>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="auto-explain-example">
  <title>Example</title>

<programlisting>
postgres=# LOAD 'auto_explain';
postgres=# SET auto_explain.log_min_duration = 0;
postgres=# SET auto_explain.log_analyze = true;
postgres=# SELECT count(*)
           FROM pg_class, pg_index
           WHERE oid = indrelid AND indisunique;
</programlisting>

  <para>
   This might produce log output such as:
  </para>

<screen><![CDATA[
LOG:  duration: 3.651 ms  plan:
  Query Text: SELECT count(*)
              FROM pg_class, pg_index
              WHERE oid = indrelid AND indisunique;
  Aggregate  (cost=16.79..16.80 rows=1 width=0) (actual time=3.626..3.627 rows=1.00 loops=1)
    ->  Hash Join  (cost=4.17..16.55 rows=92 width=0) (actual time=3.349..3.594 rows=92.00 loops=1)
          Hash Cond: (pg_class.oid = pg_index.indrelid)
          ->  Seq Scan on pg_class  (cost=0.00..9.55 rows=255 width=4) (actual time=0.016..0.140 rows=255.00 loops=1)
          ->  Hash  (cost=3.02..3.02 rows=92 width=4) (actual time=3.238..3.238 rows=92.00 loops=1)
                Buckets: 1024  Batches: 1  Memory Usage: 4kB
                ->  Seq Scan on pg_index  (cost=0.00..3.02 rows=92 width=4) (actual time=0.008..3.187 rows=92.00 loops=1)
                      Filter: indisunique
]]></screen>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="auto-explain-redaction">
  <title>Redacted Plans</title>

  <para>
   When <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname> is on, each plan is logged
   in a form intended to be shared with people who must not learn the names
   of the database's objects or the data it holds, while still showing how
   the statement was executed.  The same redaction is available interactively
   through the <literal>REDACT</literal> option of
   <xref linkend="sql-explain"/>, which is a convenient way to see what the
   log will contain.
  </para>

  <para>
   In a redacted plan:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The name of each user-defined object is replaced by a pseudonym made of
     a short prefix and a number: <literal>t1</literal> for a table, view,
     sequence or other relation, <literal>i1</literal> for an index,
     <literal>f1</literal> for a function, procedure or aggregate,
     <literal>op1</literal> for an operator, <literal>ty1</literal> for a
     type, <literal>coll1</literal> for a collation, <literal>trg1</literal>
     for a trigger, <literal>con1</literal> for a constraint,
     <literal>cte1</literal> for a <literal>WITH</literal> query,
     <literal>sp1</literal> for a subplan, <literal>w1</literal> for a
     window, <literal>fld1</literal> for a field of a composite type, and
     <literal>cur1</literal> for a cursor.  The schema of a redacted object
     is not shown.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Aliases written in the query are replaced by <literal>a1</literal>,
     <literal>a2</literal>, and so on.  A column is shown as the pseudonym or
     alias of its table followed by a column number, as in
     <literal>t1_c2</literal> or <literal>a1_c3</literal>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Every constant, including <literal>NULL</literal>, <literal>true</literal>
     and <literal>false</literal>, is replaced by <literal>?</literal>
     followed by its type, as in <literal>?::text</literal>.  Parameter
     symbols such as <literal>$1</literal> are shown as they are.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     XML functions such as <function>xmlelement</function> are shown as
     <literal>XMLEXPR(...)</literal>, the SQL/JSON query functions
     <function>JSON_EXISTS</function>, <function>JSON_QUERY</function> and
     <function>JSON_VALUE</function> as <literal>JSONEXPR(...)</literal>, and
     the table functions <function>XMLTABLE</function> and
     <function>JSON_TABLE</function> as <literal>XMLTABLE(...)</literal> and
     <literal>JSON_TABLE(...)</literal>.  Nothing inside them is shown,
     because the element, path and column names they contain are chosen by
     the user.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Objects in <literal>pg_catalog</literal> and
   <literal>information_schema</literal>, and in any schema listed in
   <varname>auto_explain.redact_allow_schemas</varname>, keep their names, so
   built-in functions, operators, types and collations appear as usual and
   the plan remains readable.
  </para>

  <para>
   Pseudonyms are assigned separately for each record and start again at 1
   in every record.  Within one record the same object always has the same
   pseudonym, so that, for example, a self-join still shows two scans of the
   same table.  Between records they identify nothing:
   <literal>t1</literal> in one record and <literal>t1</literal> in another
   need not be the same table.  The assignment depends only on the plan,
   though, so a statement that gets the same plan again gets the same
   pseudonyms again.
  </para>

  <para>
   The following are left out of a redacted plan entirely: the query text;
   the values of query parameters; the settings shown by
   <varname>auto_explain.log_settings</varname>; the query identifier; and
   everything that extensions contribute, including the output of foreign
   data wrappers (such as the remote SQL sent by
   <literal>postgres_fdw</literal>), the output and name of custom scan
   providers, and the output of <command>EXPLAIN</command> options added by
   loadable modules.  If <varname>auto_explain.log_extension_options</varname>
   is set, its options are ignored, and a message saying so is written to the
   log once per session.
  </para>

  <para>
   Everything else is kept: node types and the shape of the plan, join
   methods, estimated costs, rows and widths, and, when
   <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> is on, actual row counts,
   loops and timings, buffer, WAL and I/O usage, worker details, trigger
   timings and JIT timings.
  </para>

  <para>
   Because a redacted plan does not include its statement, each redacted
   record carries a random reference on its first line.  At the same time,
   <filename>auto_explain</filename> writes a message at level
   <literal>DEBUG1</literal> that pairs the same reference with the text of
   the statement.  For example, with
   <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname> on, a join of tables
   <structname>customers</structname> (with columns <structfield>id</structfield>,
   <structfield>name</structfield>, <structfield>ssn</structfield> and
   <structfield>region</structfield>) and <structname>orders</structname>
   might be logged as:
  </para>

<screen><![CDATA[
DEBUG:  auto_explain ref 3674e079c7cb48e4: SELECT c.name, sum(o.total) FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE c.ssn = '123-45-6789' GROUP BY c.name
LOG:  duration: 0.210 ms  ref: 3674e079c7cb48e4  plan:
  GroupAggregate  (cost=110.75..110.80 rows=1 width=36) (actual time=0.205..0.207 rows=0.00 loops=1)
    Group Key: a1.a1_c2
    ->  Sort  (cost=110.75..110.76 rows=5 width=9) (actual time=0.204..0.205 rows=0.00 loops=1)
          Sort Key: a1.a1_c2
          Sort Method: quicksort  Memory: 25kB
          ->  Hash Join  (cost=19.51..110.69 rows=5 width=9) (actual time=0.180..0.181 rows=0.00 loops=1)
                Hash Cond: (a2.a2_c2 = a1.a1_c1)
                ->  Seq Scan on t1 a2  (cost=0.00..78.00 rows=5000 width=9) (actual time=0.073..0.073 rows=1.00 loops=1)
                ->  Hash  (cost=19.50..19.50 rows=1 width=8) (actual time=0.087..0.087 rows=0.00 loops=1)
                      Buckets: 1024  Batches: 1  Memory Usage: 8kB
                      ->  Seq Scan on t2 a1  (cost=0.00..19.50 rows=1 width=8) (actual time=0.087..0.087 rows=0.00 loops=1)
                            Filter: (a1_c3 = ?::text)
                            Rows Removed by Filter: 1000
]]></screen>

  <para>
   Here <literal>t2</literal> is <structname>customers</structname>,
   <literal>a1</literal> is its alias <literal>c</literal>, and
   <literal>a1_c3</literal> is its third column, <structfield>ssn</structfield>.
   The reference is random and reveals nothing about the statement.  With
   the default setting of <xref linkend="guc-log-min-messages"/>, the
   <literal>DEBUG1</literal> message is not written at all, and records
   cannot be tied back to their statements from the log alone.  If it is
   written, it contains the full statement and must be handled like any
   other unredacted statement text; with
   <xref linkend="guc-log-destination"/> set to <literal>syslog</literal> it
   is sent at priority <literal>debug</literal>, so the syslog daemon can
   route it separately from the plans.
  </para>

  <para>
   A redacted record carries no <literal>CONTEXT</literal>, so with
   <varname>auto_explain.log_nested_statements</varname> on, a nested plan
   does not show which function ran it.  The <literal>DEBUG1</literal>
   message with the same reference keeps its <literal>CONTEXT</literal>, and
   is the way to recover that.
  </para>

  <para>
   As described under <varname>auto_explain.log_redact</varname>, redaction
   applies only to the plan records themselves; other log output, including
   the fields that <literal>csvlog</literal> and <literal>jsonlog</literal>
   add to each record, is written as usual, except that the line carrying a
   redacted plan, like each of the messages described next, shows no query
   identifier.  When a redacted plan is logged,
   <filename>auto_explain</filename> writes a message to the log once per
   session if it finds <varname>log_statement</varname> or
   <varname>log_min_duration_statement</varname> enabled, or
   <literal>DEBUG1</literal> messages being logged, since each of these puts
   statement text into the log beside the redacted plans.  These messages do
   not cover every such setting.
  </para>

  <para>
   Two limitations should be kept in mind.  The number in a column pseudonym
   reflects the column's position in its table: <literal>t1_c3</literal> is
   the third column of <literal>t1</literal>, so a reader learns the
   column's position and that the table has at least that many columns.  And
   the values of query parameters are left out entirely, not shown even as
   placeholders, whereas a constant written into the query appears with its
   type, as in <literal>?::text</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Redaction is meant to make plans safe to share.  It is not a security
   boundary against anyone who can read the data or change the server's
   configuration.  It also deliberately keeps information that is needed
   for diagnosing performance and that can still say something about the
   schema or the data:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The shape of the schema: how many tables a statement uses, whether
     they are scanned through indexes, how many partitions are scanned, and
     the number and types of the arguments of a function.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Estimated row counts and widths, which are derived from the statistics
     collected about the data.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     With <varname>auto_explain.log_analyze</varname>, actual row counts and
     timings.  These can confirm a guess: a reader who suspects the value
     hidden behind <literal>(a1_c3 = ?::text)</literal> can learn from the
     row counts whether matching rows exist.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Repetition: since a statement that gets the same plan gets the same
     pseudonyms, a reader can tell that a statement recurs.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The names of objects in <literal>pg_catalog</literal>,
     <literal>information_schema</literal> and allowlisted schemas.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Sites that pass logs on to other parties may also want to check them with
   a data-loss-prevention or personal-data detection tool before they leave.
   Such a tool is a second, independent check, able to catch text shaped like
   a sensitive value, such as an identifier, a card number or an address,
   that reached the log by some route redaction does not govern.  It is not
   a substitute for redaction, because it can only inspect what has already
   been written, whereas redaction keeps names and values from being written
   at all.  Nor does it address the retained information listed above: row
   counts, timings and plan shape do not look like personal data and pass
   such tools unchanged.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="auto-explain-author">
  <title>Author</title>

  <para>
   Takahiro Itagaki <email>itagaki.takahiro@oss.ntt.co.jp</email>
  </para>
 </sect2>

</sect1>
